Subject: FilPizlonator: pointer loads, stores and small copies for zmark_shared objects
Origin: Filnix
Upstream-Status: Not submitted

The runtime half (patches/fil-c/runtime-zmark-shared.patch) marks a shared
mapping with FILC_OBJECT_FLAG_SHARED; such an object never has an aux. The
pass's inline paths assume a null aux means "no pointers here", so:

- Pointer loads: when the aux base is null, load the aux word (monotonic) and
  test the flag; for a marked object a non-null address gets the object's own
  capability (a select, no call).
- Pointer stores: EnsureAuxPtr calls filc_object_ensure_aux_ptr_or_shared_outline,
  which returns null for a shared object; the store then goes through
  filc_store_ptr_atomic_outline, which checks the value and writes the address.
- Inline memmove: a heap side with a null aux and the flag goes to the runtime
  copy, before any byte is copied, so overlapping copies stay correct.

Loads from objects with an aux are unchanged (the address load moves into the
edge's block); loads from objects without one pay a header load, a test and a
select. Stores pay a null test of the aux base. Design, review and
measurements: filnix docs/zmark-shared.md.

